## Connect Stripe

The local demo uses a clearly marked fixture signing key. Its smoke test sends a signed HTTP webhook to `/ingest`, then checks delivery on **every expected Solace topic**. It also checks that an unsigned HTTP webhook is rejected. This proves local signed intake and routing using synthetic events; it does not contact your Stripe account.

To receive events from your Stripe test account, [install and authenticate the Stripe CLI](https://docs.stripe.com/stripe-cli), then run:

```bash
stripe listen --events payment_intent.succeeded,payment_intent.payment_failed --forward-to http://localhost:18080/ingest
```

Copy the `whsec_...` signing secret printed by that command into **STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET in .env**. Keep `stripe listen` running. In another terminal, apply the secret and trigger an event:

```bash
./scripts/install.sh --demo
stripe trigger payment_intent.succeeded
```

The listener must use the port set by MI_HTTP_PORT (default 18080). For deployed Stripe delivery, register your own publicly reachable **HTTPS /ingest** endpoint and use that endpoint's signing secret from Stripe. A reverse proxy can forward HTTPS to the local HTTP endpoint; this package does not provision hosting, DNS, or certificates. Snapshot event payloads are the default; thin events require explicit enrichment.

The fixed transport verifies HMAC-SHA256 on raw request bytes, accepts valid `v1` signatures, and checks a five-minute timestamp tolerance before invoking Core.java. Missing, changed, invalid, and stale signatures are rejected. A missing signing secret fails closed. Broker input is disabled in this webhook project; all event intake uses the signed HTTP endpoint.

Core.java sets `mi_output_topics` to the concrete topic list. The returned bytes are sent to each topic with Stripe event ID/type properties. Default mappings and examples appear in generation.json and core-examples.json. Stripe retries can produce duplicate outputs, and partial fan-out failures can duplicate an earlier publication on retry. Downstream consumers should deduplicate using stripe_event_id; this v1 does not provide a durable deduplication store or atomic multi-topic fan-out.
